  "generated_description": "<div class=\"mountain-description prose prose-stone max-w-none\">\n  <p><strong>Ptarmigan Peak</strong> is a 4,911-foot summit in Alaska. The Ptarmigan Peak Trail covers 3.1 miles round trip (out-and-back) with about 1,293 feet of elevation gain \u2014 a steady climb. It's rated Moderate overall.</p>\n  <h3>The Route</h3><p>Most of the climbing comes early \u2014 the first half of the route does the bulk of the work before the grade eases. The steepest stretch arrives around mile 0, gaining roughly 2,200 feet per mile \u2014 expect a slow, deliberate pace through here. From the trailhead at about 2,894 feet, the route tops out near 3,737 feet.</p>\n  <h3>Getting There</h3><p>The trailhead is at <a href=\"https://www.google.com/maps/dir/?api=1&destination=61.05816,-149.63302\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">61.05816, -149.63302</a> (tap for driving directions). Arrive early on weekends \u2014 trailhead lots at popular peaks fill quickly, and cell service is often unreliable once you leave the highway.</p>\n  <h3>When to Go</h3><p>Late May through October is the main season. Snow and ice linger into spring and return early in fall at this latitude and elevation; in winter this becomes a different, more serious undertaking requiring traction devices and warm layers.</p>\n  <h3>Know Before You Go</h3><p>The Moderate rating comes from the National Park Service difficulty formula, which weighs total distance against elevation gain. Most hikers should budget about 1.5 hours of moving time; add margin for breaks, photos and the descent. Download the GPX track from this page before you go \u2014 cell coverage is unreliable in the mountains and a saved route works offline in any GPS app. Conditions change: verify current access, closures and weather with the official land manager before you go. Trail data is sourced from OpenStreetMap contributors (ODbL).</p>\n  <p>Looking to link objectives or plan a weekend? Nearby summits include North Yuyanq\u2019 Ch\u2019ex, South Yuyanq\u2019 Ch\u2019ex and McHugh Peak \u2014 each has its own guide on this site.</p>\n</div>",
